 \quad\left(1-\frac{cT^2}{2L}\le\operatorname{Re}s\le1+\frac1L,
       \quad2\le|\operatorname{Im}s|\le\frac{x^3}{2}\right).$$

Let $F,G$ have the form <a href="#sh:core" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="sh:core">[sh:core]</a>, with possibly different coefficients, characters, intervals, and frequencies, subject to fixed bounds as above. For every fixed $D_0>0$ there is a fixed $B>0$ with the following property. Suppose that the sequence $\alpha$ in $F$ satisfies $$\label{sh:discrepancy}
 \left|\sum_{m\in I}\alpha_m\chi(m)m^{-1+it}\right|\leq L^{-B}
 \quad\left(
 \begin{array}{l}
 I\subset[1,x^2]\text{ an interval},\\
 \operatorname{mod}(\chi)\leq L^B,\quad |t|\leq L^B
 \end{array}\right).$$ There is no discrepancy requirement on $G$. Uniformly for $xL^{-C}\leq Z\leq xL^C$, integers $0<|k|\leq L^C$, $|a_1|,|a_2|\leq L^C$, and smooth functions $\psi$ supported in a fixed compact subinterval of $(0,\infty)$ with $\|\psi^{(j)}\|_\infty\ll_j L^{C(j+1)}$, one has $$\label{sh:correlation-bound}
 \sum_{\substack{z>0\\z+k>0}}\frac{\psi(z/Z)}z
 z^{ia_1}(z+k)^{ia_2}\overline{F(z)}G(z+k)
 W_\ell(z)W_\ell(z+k)\varepsilon(z)\varepsilon(z+k)
 \ll L^{-D_0}.$$ The exponent $B$ depends only on $D_0$ and the displayed fixed data.

The following are the Local Transference Theorem, its Endpoint Pairing Corollary, and the Absolute Physical Bounds Lemma of [[SmoothShifted](#ref-SmoothShifted), Section 3, Theorem 3.5, Corollary 3.11, and Lemma 3.4]. Assume $\sum_\nu\sup|K_\nu|\leq L^{C_1}$, where $m_0,C_1$ are fixed independently of $J$. For every fixed $E>0$ there is an $E_{\rm id}>0$, depending only on $E$ and the group data, such that $$\sup_{\Theta\in\mathbb R}
       \|\mathcal T_\zeta(\Theta)\|_{2\to2}\leq L^{-E_{\rm id}}$$ implies the following conclusion for all sufficiently large fixed $J$. Let $I=[X,2X)$ with $x^\gamma\leq X\leq x^{1/\gamma}$ for some fixed $\gamma>0$. If $f$ is supported on positions in $I$, is independent of the chosen marks, and $$\sup|f|\leq\exp(O(\sqrt L)),\qquad
 \|f\|,\|g\|\leq X^{1/2}L^{C_3},$$ then $$\label{sh:pairing}
 |\langle f,\mathcal A_\zeta g\rangle|
 \ll XL^{-E+2C_3}.$$ The lower bound for $J$ may depend on $m_0,C_1$; the threshold for $x$ may also depend on $J$, the fixed exponent bounding $|k|$, and $\gamma$. There is no additional restriction on $\zeta$ beyond the ideal norm hypothesis. The operator obtained by taking absolute values of all transition coefficients has row and column sums at most $L^{C_{\rm abs}}$, with $C_{\rm abs}$ independent of $J$.

